[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Exception Handling in C# (try/catch/finally)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Exception handling is a crucial aspect of writing robust and reliable C# applications. It allows you to gracefully handle unexpected errors or exceptional situations that may occur during program execution, preventing crashes and ensuring a better user experience. The 'try', 'catch', and 'finally' blocks are the core components of exception handling in C#."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The 'try' Block"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'try' block encloses the code that you suspect might throw an exception. It essentially marks a section of code that you want to monitor for potential errors."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"try {
  // Code that might throw an exception
  int result = 10 / 0; // This will throw a DivideByZeroException
  Console.WriteLine("Result: " + result);
} catch (Exception ex) {
  Console.WriteLine("An error occurred: " + ex.Message);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"The 'catch' Block"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'catch' block is used to handle exceptions that are thrown within the corresponding 'try' block. You can have multiple 'catch' blocks to handle different types of exceptions. When an exception occurs, the runtime searches for the first 'catch' block that can handle the specific exception type or a base type of the exception."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"try {
  // Code that might throw an exception
  int result = 10 / 0;
} catch (DivideByZeroException ex) {
  Console.WriteLine("Divide by zero error: " + ex.Message);
} catch (Exception ex) {
  Console.WriteLine("An unexpected error occurred: " + ex.Message);
}"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Specific exception types should be caught before more general exception types (e.g., 'DivideByZeroException' before 'Exception')."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If no matching 'catch' block is found, the exception will propagate up the call stack until it is handled or the program terminates."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"The 'finally' Block"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'finally' block is executed regardless of whether an exception is thrown or caught within the 'try' block. It's typically used to perform cleanup operations, such as releasing resources (e.g., closing files or database connections) or restoring the program to a consistent state. The 'finally' block guarantees that these operations will be executed even if an exception occurs."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"FileStream file = null;
try {
  file = new FileStream("example.txt", FileMode.Open);
  // Read from the file
  Console.WriteLine("File opened successfully.");
} catch (FileNotFoundException ex) {
  Console.WriteLine("File not found: " + ex.Message);
} finally {
  if (file != null) {
    file.Close();
    Console.WriteLine("File closed.");
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Exception Filters (C# 6.0 and later)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Exception filters allow you to conditionally catch exceptions based on certain criteria. This can be useful when you want to handle specific cases of an exception without catching all instances of that exception type."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"try {
  // Code that might throw an exception
  throw new ArgumentException("Invalid argument", "parameterName");
} catch (ArgumentException ex) when (ex.ParamName == "parameterName") {
  Console.WriteLine("Invalid argument 'parameterName': " + ex.Message);
} catch (ArgumentException ex) {
  Console.WriteLine("Another argument exception: " + ex.Message);
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Only catch exceptions that you can handle meaningfully. Avoid catching exceptions just to re-throw them."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use specific exception types whenever possible to provide more targeted error handling."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Log exceptions to help diagnose and resolve issues."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the 'finally' block to ensure that resources are properly released, even if an exception occurs."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider using exception filters to handle specific cases of exceptions."},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"By following these guidelines, you can write more robust and maintainable C# applications that gracefully handle errors and provide a better user experience."}]
